---
<Layout title="About DNP" description="What DNP: Did Not Plan is, what it publishes about the league, and the house rules: verified recaps only, no manager identities, unknown never becomes zero." active="about">
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="kicker"><span class="flag">About DNP</span><span>Rules of the court</span></p>
    <h1>One league. <mark>Zero chill.</mark> Strict rules.</h1>
    <p class="page-dek">DNP: Did Not Plan is the blog of a single NBA Redraft league. It covers that league and nothing else, in the voice of a tabloid that thinks it is a courtroom.</p>
  </header>
  <Tape text="House rules in effect · Please remain seated" tilt="right" />
  <div class="about wrap">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="what-gets-filed">What gets filed</h2>
      <p><strong>Weekly recaps.</strong> After a matchup week ends and its results are verified, the DNP Desk files a recap for that week. Each recap lives in the archive under its season and week number, with earlier and later links to walk the season in order.</p>
      <p><strong>Clubhouse notes.</strong> Occasional notes from the league desk, like the opening note. They sit apart from the weekly recaps and are never numbered as a matchup week.</p>
      <p>That is the whole menu. DNP does not publish general fantasy advice, player rankings, or NBA news.</p>
      <h2 id="house-rules">House rules</h2>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Verified or it didn't happen.</strong> A weekly recap is filed only from verified league data for that week. Every recap names who verified it and when. If a week's data is incomplete, the week waits.</li>
        <li><strong>Teams, not people.</strong> No manager names, usernames, contact details, or private league links appear here. Private league identifiers and invite links are blocked by the build itself.</li>
        <li><strong>Unknown is not zero.</strong> A number that has not been verified is left out and labeled as unknown. It is never filled in with a zero or a guess.</li>
        <li><strong>No invented anything.</strong> No made-up quotes, trades, scores, injuries, or drama. The jokes are opinions. The facts are checked.</li>
        <li><strong>The robot testifies.</strong> The DNP Desk writes with AI assistance and says so on every filing, under “The record.”</li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="how-it-is-built">How it is built</h2>
      <p>Every filing is a plain text file with its season, week, and verification details. When a new verified week is added, the front page, the weekly archive, the feed, and the sitemap update on their own. Nobody redesigns anything to publish week 9.</p>
      <p>Each filing ends with “The record”: who filed it, when, whether and by whom it was verified, and a list of public exhibits. Private league material used to verify a recap stays private. It is described, never linked.</p>
      <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
      <p>If a filing turns out to be wrong, it gets fixed. The court may be petty, but it is not stubborn.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
